Find the area of the hexagon using two trapeziums. Then check it using a rectangle and two triangles.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.4,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (2,0)--(8,0)--(10,4)--(8,8)--(2,8)--(0,4)--cycle;
\path (5,0) node[below] {$6\text{ cm}$};
\path (5,8) node[above] {$6\text{ cm}$};
\draw[dashed] (0,4)--(10,4);
\path (5,4) node[above,fill=white] {$10\text{ cm}$};
\draw[<->] (11,0)--(11,4) node[midway,right] {$4\text{ cm}$};
\draw[<->] (11,4)--(11,8) node[midway,right] {$4\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The top endpoints are directly above the bottom endpoints. The tips lie halfway up the figure.

A rectangle is joined along one full side of a rhombus. The rhombus has diagonals $12\text{ cm}$ and $16\text{ cm}$ and side length $10\text{ cm}$. The rectangle's shared side is $10\text{ cm}$ long, and its perpendicular width is $3\text{ cm}$. Find the total area.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.25,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-8,0)--(0,6)--(1.8,8.4)--(9.8,2.4)--(8,0)--(0,-6)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,6)--(8,0);
\draw[dashed] (-8,0)--(8,0);
\draw[dashed] (0,-6)--(0,6);
\path (-4,0) node[below] {$16\text{ cm}$};
\path (0,-3) node[right] {$12\text{ cm}$};
\path (5.8,5.4) node[above right] {$10\text{ cm}$};
\path (8.9,1.2) node[right] {$3\text{ cm}$};
\draw (7.6,0.3)--(7.9,0.7)--(8.3,0.4);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
